# Draft reply — committee thread (SUPA proposal circulated)

**Audience:** reply-all to the committee thread. Nick's own voice, not a point-by-point response to other members.
**Goal:** welcome the second offer as a market signal, establish the two proposals aren't equivalent and why, define what to ask both providers, float the intercom add-on, then propose a meeting once both responses are in.
**Subject:** RE: 728653T - 40-44 Pakington Street St Kilda - Feasibility study on the installation of EV Chargers

---

Hi all,

It's great that we now have a second offer on the table. More than anything it's a really good indication of what's actually available in the market, and a good example of why it pays not to just settle on the first quote we were given. Having two providers engaged puts us in a much stronger position.

The one thing I'd flag is that the Origin and SUPA proposals aren't equivalent, so we shouldn't compare them on headline price alone. Origin is quoting us EV backbone infrastructure as a one-off capital cost to the OC that future-proofs all of our car spaces, with no energy supply contract attached. SUPA is proposing to take over our electricity, hot water and metering as our embedded network operator, and to bundle three shared EV chargers into that arrangement. The "$0 upfront" is appealing, but it isn't free. SUPA recovers that cost, plus the annual credit, through the margin on our energy and hot water over the life of the contract, so it's really an energy and hot water supply contract with chargers attached rather than free EV infrastructure. On the EV side specifically they also solve different problems: Origin's backbone lets any of our car spaces add a charger later with the owner paying their own install, whereas SUPA's three shared chargers don't make the building EV-ready in the same way unless their install also leaves capacity for owners to add chargers at their own bays down the track.

To compare them properly I think we need a bit more in writing from both providers.

From SUPA:
- The agreement term, and how long the discounts and the credit are locked in.
- Whether their installation leaves capacity for individual owners to add a charger at their own space later, how many the building can support, and what that would cost an owner.
- What residents pay to use the three shared chargers, and how that is billed.
- How their electricity and hot water rates compare in actual dollars to what we pay now, not just as a discount off the default offer.
- The exit terms, and who owns the meters and chargers at the end of the agreement.

From Origin:
- A firm quote rather than the indicative number, and clarity on whether their load management and per-charger subscription lock us into Origin for billing.
- Confirmation that the OC would own the backbone outright, and that the proposal carries no tie to Origin as our energy or hot water supplier.

While we have SUPA's attention and the negotiating power that comes with our whole embedded network, it might also be worth asking them what else they can fold in. SUPA's sister companies offer other building services, including intercoms, which is a known issue for us. If we could bundle an intercom upgrade or a subsidised solution into the same arrangement, it would solve another problem for the building and make the whole project more appealing to a wider group of lot owners.

Once we've got those responses back from both Origin and SUPA, my suggestion would be that we get together for a meeting to go through them side by side and decide how we want to proceed.

Thanks,
Nick
